// =============================================================================
// evictions-course — "Evictions Handled Righteously: What the Word Restrains
// When Somebody Cannot Pay"
// =============================================================================
// Course ELEVEN of the Real Estate department. Ten courses covered the ground,
// the stewardship, the transaction, the tenant, the building, the partner, the
// lender, the record, the loss and the looking. This one covers the hardest
// hour any of them can produce: the day somebody in your building cannot pay,
// and you are the one holding the power.
//
// THE ARC, one argument in eight moves:
//   1. They turn the needy out of the way   (Job 24:2-4, 7, 9, 12)
//   2. Cast out from their pleasant houses  (Micah 2:9, 10)
//   3. Thou shalt stand abroad              (Deuteronomy 24:10-13)
//   4. He taketh a man's life to pledge     (Deuteronomy 24:6, 17, 18)
//   5. The year to redeem                   (Leviticus 25:29-33)
//   6. Took him by the throat               (Matthew 18:23-35)
//   7. Job's own oath                       (Job 31:13, 15, 16, 21, 32, 38, 39)
//   8. Restored to the debtor his pledge    (Ezekiel 18:7, 16; Amos 2:6-8)
//
// See that Scripture looks straight at people being put out and describes it in
// detail -> hear Yahweh Himself name it as a charge -> get the restraint He put
// on the CREDITOR'S METHOD while leaving the debt standing -> learn the things
// that may never be seized at all -> find a redemption window that is real and
// bounded at BOTH ends -> stand in front of the parable that indicts a forgiven
// man who would not extend what he had received -> put yourself under the oath
// before you put anybody out -> and close on the man Ezekiel calls righteous,
// who is known partly by what he GAVE BACK.
//
// WHAT THIS COURSE IS NOT, and it has to be said at the top because the subject
// attracts abuse from both directions. It is not a licence to put a family into
// the street, and it is not a claim that no lawful remedy exists. Scripture
// does not abolish the debt anywhere in this material -- the pledge is still
// taken in Deuteronomy 24:10, the walled-city house is genuinely lost in
// Leviticus 25:30, and the lord in Matthew 18 had a real claim. What Scripture
// does, over and over, is restrain the METHOD and the MEMORY of the one holding
// the power. That is the course.
//
// THE FOUR LIMITS THIS COURSE HOLDS, each a place the material is easily
// abused, and each pinned by the course test rather than trusted to prose:
//
//   Job 24 -- this is JOB SPEAKING inside the dispute, not a pronouncement from
//     Yahweh. The description of oppression is accurate and the Word preserved
//     it, which is why it opens the course. But the clause at the end of 24:12
//     -- "yet God layeth not folly to them" -- is Job's complaint from inside
//     his suffering, and the whole book is the answer to it. It must never be
//     taught as a doctrine about what Yahweh does or does not charge.
//   Deuteronomy 24:10-13 -- the restraint is on METHOD, not on the debt. The
//     pledge is still taken; the creditor simply may not cross the threshold to
//     get it, and may not keep a poor man's covering overnight. Teaching this
//     as "no one may ever collect" reverses the text.
//   Matthew 18:23-35 -- this is forgiveness between servants of the King, not a
//     landlord-tenant statute. The lord's claim was lawful, the hundred pence
//     was really owed, and the parable ends with the wicked servant delivered
//     to the tormentors. What it indicts is a forgiven man refusing to extend
//     what he had received.
//   Leviticus 25:29-30 -- the law states its OWN limit, and anyone who stops at
//     verse 29 misreads it. A walled-city house not redeemed within the full
//     year "shall be established for ever to him that bought it throughout his
//     generations: it shall not go out in the jubile" (25:30). A real and
//     permanent transfer, inside the jubilee law itself.
//
// FRESHNESS, MEASURED BEFORE A WORD WAS WRITTEN AND RE-MEASURED AFTER IT WAS
// MOUNTED (DR-0076 sec.4), in the order course ten learned and course nine paid
// for. The candidate pool of 59 fetched verses was run against the department's
// ten other mounted courses before authoring; the finished course cites 43 of
// them, and the numbers below are from the second measurement, taken against
// the mounted file rather than the plan. The first draft of this header said 59
// and named a verse the course does not cite. That is exactly how course nine's
// claim went stale, and it was caught the same way -- by measuring again.
//
// What is true, measured: this course cites 43 verses and NOT ONE of them is
// cited anywhere else in the Real Estate department. FOUR chapters are shared,
// each at different verses, and every one of them is named here so the claim
// cannot quietly widen:
//   Deuteronomy 24 -- here :6, :10-13, :17-18; property-principle at :14-15
//   Leviticus 25   -- here :29-33; property-principle at :10, :15, :16, :23, :25
//   Matthew 18     -- here :25-30, :33, :35; leasing-tenants at :15
//   Micah 2        -- here :9-10; property-principle at :1-2 (Naboth's vineyard)
// That is more shared chapters than course ten's one, and it is stated plainly
// rather than dressed down, because the chapters that carry pledge, redemption
// and the unpayable debt are finite and this department has been working them
// for eleven courses.
//
// THREE VERSES ARE SHARED WITH COURSES IN OTHER DEPARTMENTS, named here so the
// claim above is not mistaken for a wider one than it is: Job 31:15 (the
// sovereign-ai class), Matthew 18:33 (Living Lessons) and Amos 2:6 (Living
// Lessons and world-issues). The department pin is verse-level zero; the
// catalog-wide picture is these three, and the course test pins both.
//
// THREE PASSAGES WERE RULED OUT BY THE SAME MEASUREMENT before they were
// written: 2 Kings 4:1-7, the widow whose sons were about to be taken for the
// debt, is already the anchor of fin5; Nehemiah 5, where the people had
// mortgaged their fields to buy corn, is already mgmt7 and bank3; and James 5's
// cry of the reapers is already prop7 and econ6. Each would have been a natural
// lesson here. Each was already mounted, so each died before authoring.
//
// BANDS, BENEFITS AND STORIES FROM THE FIRST COMMIT (DR-0509), every hard term
// leads with its plain meaning (DR-0521), and every quoted span is verbatim
// from the repository's own KJV with its own reference and no ellipsis
// (DR-0459). Our prose says Yahweh; quotations keep "God" and "the LORD"
// exactly as the KJV has them (DR-0210).
//
// TEACHING, NOT LEGAL ADVICE -- carried on the meta, and it matters more in
// this course than in any other in the department, because the wrong move here
// is both a sin and in most places a crime.
// =============================================================================

