    title: 'Cast out from their pleasant houses — this time it is a charge, and Yahweh is the one bringing it',
    bigIdea: 'Lesson one was a man describing what he saw. This is Yahweh speaking through His prophet, and it is not a description — it is an indictment, addressed directly to the people who did it. "The women of my people have ye cast out from their pleasant houses; from their children have ye taken away my glory for ever." (Micah 2:9) Read the pronoun. YE cast out. He is not narrating a misfortune, He is naming a defendant. And He describes what the children lost in a way nobody would have chosen on their own: not safety, not stability — MY GLORY. Then the next verse hands the very words of dispossession back to the same YE: "Arise ye, and depart; for this is not your rest: because it is polluted, it shall destroy you, even with a sore destruction." (Micah 2:10) Arise and depart is what you say to somebody you are putting out. In verse 10 it is being said to the ones who said it.',
    inApp: 'Take the last removal you were part of, or the one you are contemplating, and write the sentence of Micah 2:9 with your own name in the pronoun slot. Not as self-flagellation and not as a verdict — as a test. Read it back and ask honestly whether it fits or whether it does not. There are removals this sentence does not fit and this course will say so plainly in lesson three and lesson six. But you cannot know which one you are holding until you have written it out with your name in it and looked at it. Then write one line naming what the children in that household lost, using nouns rather than adjectives.',
    anchor: { ref: 'Micah 2:9; Micah 2:10', theme: 'A charge in the second person, brought by Yahweh through His prophet against His own people, and its subject is housing. "The women of my people have ye cast out from their pleasant houses; from their children have ye taken away my glory for ever." (Micah 2:9) The verb is theirs and the pronoun is direct: ye cast out. The loss to the children is not named in human terms at all — He calls it His own glory, taken away, for ever. And the sentence that follows uses the language of eviction against the evictors: "Arise ye, and depart; for this is not your rest: because it is polluted, it shall destroy you, even with a sore destruction." (Micah 2:10) The same ye that cast out is now told to arise and depart, and told that the place it thought was rest is not.' },
    levels: {
      teen: 'In the last lesson a suffering man described people being put out of their homes. This lesson is different in a way you should not miss. Here the one speaking is Yahweh, through a prophet named Micah, and He is not describing anything. He is pressing a charge. Look at how it is worded. "The women of my people have ye cast out from their pleasant houses; from their children have ye taken away my glory for ever." (Micah 2:9) Find the word YE. That is the old English word for you, plural. He is not saying it happened. He is saying YOU did it, to MY people. Now look at the two things He says were taken. The first is the houses, and He calls them PLEASANT houses, which is a small word doing a lot of work. He did not say adequate. He did not say shelter. The house was pleasant, meaning somebody liked being in it, and that is what was taken. The second thing is stranger and much bigger. He says that from the children, YE have taken away MY GLORY for ever. He does not say the children lost security. He does not say they lost their childhood. He calls what was taken His own glory. That is Yahweh saying that something belonging to Him was attached to those children being in that house, and that removing them removed it. He does not explain it any further than that, and neither will we — where the Word stops, we stop. Now the next verse, and it is one of the sharpest turns in the prophets. "Arise ye, and depart; for this is not your rest: because it is polluted, it shall destroy you, even with a sore destruction." (Micah 2:10) Arise and depart is what a person says when they are putting you out. Get up. Go. And it is exactly what is now being said back, to the same YE from the verse before. The people who told families to get up and go are told to get up and go. And the reason given is that the place they thought was their rest is not their rest at all. So here is what you carry out of this lesson. Putting people out of housing is not a neutral business matter that Yahweh watches from a distance. In at least one place in Scripture He calls it by name, in the second person, to people who thought of themselves as His, and He says what was taken from the children belonged to Him.',
      senior: 'Set this lesson directly against the last one and name the difference before you read, because the difference is the whole point. Job 24 was a suffering man describing dispossession from inside his own ruin. Micah 2 is Yahweh speaking through His prophet, and He is not describing — He is charging. Read the verse and make the room find the pronoun. "The women of my people have ye cast out from their pleasant houses; from their children have ye taken away my glory for ever." (Micah 2:9) Ye cast out. Second person, plural, addressed to His own people, about housing. There is no distance in it and no passive voice to hide behind. Then take the two losses separately, because they are not the same kind of loss. The first is the houses, and the adjective is worth a minute: PLEASANT houses. Not adequate, not sufficient. Somebody liked living there, and that liking is part of what the charge names as taken. Anyone who has ever reduced a family to a housing unit in their own mind should sit with that word. The second loss is the harder one and it is the reason this verse sits in this course. From the children, He says, ye have taken away MY GLORY for ever. He does not describe the loss in human terms at all. He does not say the children lost stability or safety, both of which are true and both of which we would have reached for. He says that what was removed was His own glory, and He puts no explanation after it. Teach exactly that and stop there. The Word is reticent here and we will be reticent with it; building a doctrine in the silence is not teaching, it is invention. What can be said, and should be said with force, is this: Yahweh treats the removal of these households as a matter touching His own glory, which means it is not an administrative event He is watching from a height. Then verse 10, and it is one of the sharpest reversals in the prophets. "Arise ye, and depart; for this is not your rest: because it is polluted, it shall destroy you, even with a sore destruction." (Micah 2:10) Arise and depart is eviction language. It is what one says to the person being put out. In verse 10 it is being said to the same ye who said it in verse 9, and the ground given is that the rest they believed they were standing on is not rest. The surrounding chapter is an indictment of men who coveted what was not theirs and took it, so the sentence is not arbitrary; it is the thing they did, handed back. Close by making the room state, in their own words, what changes about a removal once you know Yahweh is willing to speak of it in the second person.',
    },
