    lesson: 'Set this lesson against the last one and name the difference out loud before reading a word, because the difference carries the lesson. Job 24 was a suffering man describing dispossession from inside his own ruin. Micah 2 is Yahweh speaking through His prophet, and He is not describing. He is charging. "The women of my people have ye cast out from their pleasant houses; from their children have ye taken away my glory for ever." (Micah 2:9) Make the room find the pronoun before you say anything else. YE cast out. Second person, plural, addressed to His own covenant people, about housing specifically. There is no distance in the sentence and no passive construction to shelter behind. It is not a market outcome and it is not a regrettable necessity; it is an act with a doer, and He is naming the doer. Now take the two losses separately, because they are not the same kind of thing. The first is the houses, and the adjective deserves a full minute: PLEASANT houses. He did not say adequate. He did not say sufficient shelter. Somebody liked being there, and that liking is inside what the charge says was taken. Anybody who has ever let a home become a unit in their own vocabulary should sit still under that word for a moment. The second loss is the harder one, and it is why this verse belongs in this course at all. From the children, He says, ye have taken away MY GLORY for ever. He does not describe the loss in human terms. He does not say they lost stability, or safety, or a childhood, all of which are true and all of which we would have reached for first. He says that what was taken away from those children was His own glory, and He puts no explanation after it. Teach exactly that much and then stop. The Word is reticent here, and we will be reticent with it; constructing a doctrine inside the silence would be invention, not teaching. What CAN be said, and should be said with weight, is this: Yahweh is willing to speak about the removal of these households as a matter touching His own glory. That settles the question of whether this is an administrative event He watches from a height. It is not. Then verse 10, which is one of the sharpest reversals anywhere in the prophets. "Arise ye, and depart; for this is not your rest: because it is polluted, it shall destroy you, even with a sore destruction." (Micah 2:10) Arise and depart is eviction language. It is precisely what one person says to another while putting them out. Get up. Go. And here it is being said to the same ye from the verse before — the grammar is plain, the pronoun does not change — with the ground given that the rest they believed themselves to be standing on is not rest at all. The chapter around it indicts men who coveted what was not theirs and took it, so the sentence is not arbitrary. It is the thing they did, handed back to them in their own words. Close the session by making each person say, in their own words, what changes about a removal once they know Yahweh is willing to speak of it in the second person. Then send them to the in-app task, because the next three lessons are rules, and rules land differently on somebody who has already written their own name into verse 9 and looked at it.',
