    lesson: 'Lesson three governed the method of taking a pledge. This lesson names a category that sits outside the system altogether — things that may not be taken at any price, for any debt, under any circumstance. Establish the object first and plainly, because a room that does not know what a millstone is will hear this passage as quaint antiquity. A household ground its own grain between two stones: a heavy stationary one below and a turning one above. Grain goes in, flour comes out, there is bread that day. It is not equipment and it is not a luxury. It is the kitchen, and the family eats out of it daily. "No man shall take the nether or the upper millstone to pledge: for he taketh a man’s life to pledge." (Deuteronomy 24:6) Nether is the old word for lower. Two things in that verse deserve the room’s whole attention. FIRST, BOTH STONES ARE NAMED, and the reason is that only one of them is needed to end the mill. A creditor could take the upper stone alone and argue, with perfect truthfulness, that he had left the larger and more valuable object exactly where it was. Yahweh closes the clever route and the crude one in a single breath, and that is worth noticing about how He legislates — He forecloses the workaround rather than waiting to be outmanoeuvred by it. Ask the room what the modern upper stone is, and let them name their own trade’s version of it, because every trade has one. SECOND, THE REASON IS STATED. The law does not often trouble to explain itself and here it does, and what it says reclassifies the whole act. Taking the instrument of a household’s daily bread is not the seizure of a possession. He calls it taking the man’s life to pledge. Not endangering his life, not harming his livelihood — taking his life, as a pledge, the way you would take an object and hold it. Stay in that sentence until the room feels the category move. Then verse 17, where the protection strengthens rather than weakens as exposure increases: "Thou shalt not pervert the judgment of the stranger, nor of the fatherless; nor take a widow’s raiment to pledge:" (Deuteronomy 24:17) Raiment is clothing. Set that deliberately beside lesson three and let the contrast do the teaching. A poor man’s covering COULD be taken as a pledge, on the condition that it came back at every sundown. A widow’s covering may not be taken in the first place. The protection is strongest exactly where the person has least ability to defend herself, and that inverts the behaviour of nearly every human system ever built: ordinarily, the less power a party holds, the harder the terms they are handed. Here it runs the other way, on purpose, by command. Finally, the enforcement mechanism, and it is not a penalty clause. "But thou shalt remember that thou wast a bondman in Egypt, and the LORD thy God redeemed thee thence: therefore I command thee to do this thing." (Deuteronomy 24:18) A bondman is a slave. Yahweh does not say obey this or a fine follows. He anchors the restraint in memory: you were the one with no power, and you were redeemed by somebody who was under no obligation to do it. THEREFORE. A man who genuinely remembers what he was does not require a statute to stop him taking a widow’s coat, and a man who has forgotten will find a way around any statute you write. Close on the practical translation and make the room say it out loud rather than saying it for them: before anything is taken, name what the thing is FOR rather than what it is worth. The work van. The tools. The telephone the job calls. The car that carries the children to school. The machine that keeps four children in clean clothes. Those are millstones, and they are not on the table at any figure, in any negotiation, for any amount owed.',
