        title: 'I Stretched It For The Ones I Liked',
        body: 'I want to say the thing this lesson made me see, because it was not the thing I expected. I never set a hard date with anybody. I thought that made me the generous one. We had six units and I would tell people to catch up when they could and I genuinely meant it, and I believed for years that this was the kind way to run it. Then a man asked me a question I could not answer. He asked how long I let people run. And when I actually went back through it, the answer was that I let a couple who came to our church run nineteen months, and I put a young man with a temper out at eleven weeks, and both of those decisions felt entirely reasonable to me at the time and I could have defended each one separately. Put side by side they are indefensible. I had no window, so what I actually had was my own feelings about people, applied at whatever moment I had had enough. The couple were not more deserving. They were easier to be around. Yahweh said a whole year, and then He said a full year, and then He said what happens when it runs out, and He said all of it in advance, to everybody, the same. I now set a number and a date with every single person before any trouble starts, and the people it protects most are the ones I do not particularly like.',
