    lesson: 'This is the close of the course and it arrives as two portraits set facing one another. Start with Ezekiel, and build the expectation before you read so the surprise actually lands: the prophet is drawing a righteous man, and a room primed to hear a list of devotional practices is about to receive something quite different. "And hath not oppressed any, but hath restored to the debtor his pledge, hath spoiled none by violence, hath given his bread to the hungry, and hath covered the naked with a garment;" (Ezekiel 18:7) Read the sequence a second time and then ask the room a single question: where is the business section? There is not one. Restoring a debtor’s pledge sits between not oppressing and feeding the hungry, inside one unbroken list of what righteousness is. THE PLACEMENT IS THE ARGUMENT. A man’s conduct as a creditor is not a separate compartment of his life to be assessed under separate rules, with the spiritual part evaluated elsewhere on Sunday. It is on the same list, in the same breath, as whether he fed anybody. Then show the standard holding across a generation, because Ezekiel draws the portrait again for a righteous son of an unrighteous father, and this item survives the redrawing, stated as a refusal rather than as an act: "Neither hath oppressed any, hath not withholden the pledge, neither hath spoiled by violence, but hath given his bread to the hungry, and hath covered the naked with a garment," (Ezekiel 18:16) Restored, in the first portrait. Not withholden, in the second. The positive and the negative of one obligation, a generation apart, and the standard does not move between them. Now turn the room to the facing portrait, which is the one that closes the course. Amos opens his charge against Israel by naming a price. "Thus saith the LORD; For three transgressions of Israel, and for four, I will not turn away the punishment thereof; because they sold the righteous for silver, and the poor for a pair of shoes;" (Amos 2:6) A pair of shoes is the figure named for a human being, and it is worth letting the room sit with how small a sum a person can be traded for once he has been reclassified as a line. Then the charge that reaches the poor directly: "That pant after the dust of the earth on the head of the poor, and turn aside the way of the meek:" (Amos 2:7) Say plainly that the verse continues with further transgressions this course is not treating, so that nobody leaves thinking the clause was the whole verse. And then give them the last one, and let it stand with no decoration at all: "And they lay themselves down upon clothes laid to pledge by every altar, and they drink the wine of the condemned in the house of their god." (Amos 2:8) Take them straight back to lesson three. A poor man’s pledge was ordinarily his outer garment, which was also his covering at night, and Yahweh required it returned at every sundown so that the man might sleep in his own raiment and bless the lender, and He called that righteousness. Here are men reclining ON those garments. By every altar. In the posture of worship, in the place where worship is conducted. The covering owed back to a cold man is underneath a worshipper. Say it once and do not add to it. THIS IS WHERE THE COURSE CLOSES, AND IT CLOSES WHERE IT OPENED. Job 24 showed people caused to lodge without clothing, with no covering in the cold. Amos 2:8 shows who is lying on the covering, and where. Then give the send-off without rushing it, gathering the eight moves: look straight at it rather than at the paperwork; hear that Yahweh is willing to bring this as a charge in the second person; stand outside the door and let the man bring the pledge out; never take the millstone, whatever you are owed; state the window at both ends in advance and honour both ends; remember what you were forgiven when you hear your own sentence in somebody else’s mouth; put yourself under the oath before you put anybody out; and give back what was laid in pledge. Then close on the one task in front of each person tonight — the list of what they are holding that is not theirs, and the return of everything in the second column this week rather than after the dispute resolves. And for anybody in the room who is the one behind and frightened rather than the one holding the power: you are allowed to ask for what is yours, plainly and in writing, and lesson three is about what may not be done to you.',
